# Introducing HTML

####  Now that you have been introduced to user experience, let’s dive into how the web apps are made.

## HTML

#### **HTML (HyperText Markup Language)** is the code that gives a web page its structure. It tells the browser what to show — like headings, paragraphs, images, and links — so users can see and interact with the content.


>#### Look at the HTML below. Can you imagine what parts of the web page might look like?


<table style="width:100%;margin:20px 0;border-collapse:collapse;">
<tr>

<td style="vertical-align:top;text-align:center;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/1a60c7acc114352abd9a7e83320ddaa9-image-1763396009343.png" alt="html code with head, body, header, main and footer labeled" width="900" style="border-radius:10px;">
</td>

<td style="width:350px;vertical-align:top;text-align:center;padding-top:20px;">
<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:14px 22px;font-size:18px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
Click to reveal the web page.
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:16px;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/755f257f750cad47ad5323718eff8e78-Screenshot%202026-01-15%20at%203.38.24%20PM.png" alt="annotated html structure" width="450" style="border-radius:10px;">
</div>
</details>
</td>

</tr>
</table>




## The Structure of an HTML Page

- #### **The `<head>` Section** -The head holds information about the page, not what’s shown on the page itself.

- #### **The `<body>` Section** is what users see on the webpage.
	- #### The `<header>` is the top portion of a website.
    - #### The `<main>` section holds the core content of the site. In this example, there is a `<section>` with a subheading (`<h2>`), a paragraph (`<p>`), and a list (`<ul>`with `<li>` items).
    - #### The `<footer>` section closes out the web page. This example has a copyright line. 
    
## Next Up

#### Let's take a look at some common HTML tags. 

